# Cover letter draft

Dear Editor,

Please consider our manuscript, “A Reproducible Subject-Independent Comparison of Compact CNN, CNN–BiLSTM and CNN–Transformer Models for Single-Channel Sleep Staging,” as a research comparison/reproducibility study.

We compare established compact model families on PhysioNet Sleep-EDF Expanded using a frozen subject-independent split, fixed preprocessing and validation-only checkpoint selection. All {{selected_checkpoints}} preselected final checkpoints evaluate the identical {{test_epochs}} epochs. Subject-aware paired bootstrap intervals are reported separately from training-seed variability. The contextual models improve aggregate macro-F1 relative to the preserved CNN reference, while the direct contextual-model interval does not establish a reliable advantage. N1 and transition results qualify the aggregate findings.

The manuscript’s contribution is its controlled protocol, explicit epoch/checkpoint lineage and reproducible analysis package. It does not claim architectural novelty, state-of-the-art performance or clinical effectiveness. We disclose the baseline’s single seed, the contextual models’ three seeds, a small final subject cohort and the difference in available temporal context. All historical results remain preserved, and the already-used test set is closed to further development.

The package includes publication figures, complete individual-seed tables, verified references and a reproducibility checklist. We believe the work may be useful to readers interested in trustworthy single-channel EEG model comparisons and practical research infrastructure.

Sincerely,

[Corresponding author name, affiliation and contact details — to be supplied by the authors]

